# /test-plugin — run and judge the plugin contract suites
## Run
```bash
node "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/run_suites.js" # all suites
node "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/run_suites.js" audit # selected suite only
```
## Exit codes
| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | Every suite that ran passed |
| 1 | One or more suites failed (failed checks are included) |
| 2 | **No suite was available to run** |
Code 2 is essential. Treating zero passing checks as 0 would report “verification
success” even after the entire tree disappeared. A missing check did not pass.
This command checks the plugin's own contract suites. Its target differs from
`/validate`, which checks the format and content of user-created deliverables.
Frozen references are compared with Git blobs, so run the complete check in a
development checkout. An installation cache without `.git` can run package and
workflow runtime checks, but must not claim success for the full source suite.
## When a suite fails
Read the failed checks as printed. Their names state what they lock, which usually
identifies the broken contract without further interpretation.
When intentionally changing the contract count, update `check_total` as well. It
is a **change detector**, not a measurement; auto-adjusting it would detect nothing.
